import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About — mazetick"
  description="mazetick publishes time-stamped market data for UK and Irish racing, and the measurements behind it. No tips, no promised returns."
>
  <h1 class="display title">About</h1>

  <div class="prose body">
    <p>
      mazetick is a data site for UK and Irish horse racing. The product is not a
      prediction and not a selection. It is the <strong>market layer, stamped with
      the time it was true</strong> — a record of what could be known at each hour of
      the day.
    </p>

    <h2>Why that, and not a card</h2>
    <p>
      Racing Post, Sporting Life and At The Races already publish the racecard, the
      form and the prices, free, and better than we would. Reproducing them would add
      nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      What none of them publishes is <em>how any of it moved through the day</em>.
      Which each-way terms a bookmaker was advertising at 10:00, and again at 14:00.
      How wide the book was when you looked. Whether the going changed after the
      04:00 declaration. Those facts exist only if somebody is recording them
      continuously, and once the day is over they cannot be recovered. So we record
      them, every hour, and keep them.
    </p>

    <h2>What we will not do</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>We do not sell selections and we do not promise a return.</strong> That
      is not modesty. It is the result of two years of trying to beat these markets
      with machine learning and with classic handicapping rules, and measuring
      honestly enough to find that neither worked. The market price already contains
      what we knew, and more.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those failures are published here rather than buried, in{" "}
      <a href="/research">Research</a>, with their sample sizes. A site that tells you
      a bet winning 78.8% of the time still loses money is a site that has no reason
      to flatter the next number it shows you.
    </p>

    <h2>How the numbers are made</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Every figure carries its timestamp.</strong> A table without an "as
        of" is somebody else's product, not ours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No look-ahead.</strong> A statistic about a race uses only information
        that existed before it was run. This sounds obvious and is the single most
        common way racing statistics are overstated — including, at times, our own.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sample and window on the page.</strong> If a rate is computed from
        twelve runs, it says twelve.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Negative results get published.</strong> They are the most defensible
        thing we own.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>
      Bookmaker each-way terms are read from the bookmaker's own public pages, hourly.
      Exchange prices and book depth come from the Smarkets public API. Race records
      and results come from a licensed racing data feed. Derived statistics — strike
      rates by going, by course, by distance — are our own calculations over those
      records, and are described where they appear.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Corrections are welcome, particularly to the measurements.{" "}
      <a href="/contact">Get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .title { font-size: var(--fs-display-l); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }
  .body { margin-top: var(--sp-8); }
</style>
